<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">

    <section class="section" id="intro">
    <h2><span class="num">01 — Context</span>They Asked for It</h2>

    <p>"They have asked the government to tie them to the mast." The sentence appears on page one of a motion filed September 28, 2026, in the Circuit Court of Highlands County, Florida, asking a single county judge to bar OpenAI from developing new artificial intelligence models without "independent third-party guardrails and approval." The evidence for the request was almost entirely OpenAI's own words.</p>

    <p>Three weeks earlier, on September 6, OpenAI chief scientist Jakub Pachocki published a blog post titled "An Alien Mind." In it he wrote: "This is a time that calls for extreme caution. I am concerned no one is prepared for the consequences of a continued rapid rise in machine intelligence. ... I believe broader interventions are required." Two days later, Jacob Coxon — described in the motion as a former OpenAI researcher who worked at the company from 2020 to 2024 — posted on X that OpenAI was "gambling with our lives," that "many [at OpenAI] have not deeply internalized the civilizational stakes," and that "[n]o other human activity poses this level of danger." Evan Hubinger, whom the motion describes as a former OpenAI employee who now heads product safety at Anthropic, responded the same day: he "earnestly believe[s] AI could kill all humans! I personally think it is >10% within the next decade."</p>

    <p>On September 9, Paul Christiano — OpenAI's newest board member — published a statement: "... I now believe there is a meaningful risk that rapid acceleration in AI capabilities leads to catastrophic and irreversible loss of control in the very near term. I do not think ... OpenAI[] is currently on track to reduce this risk to an acceptable level."</p>

