    <p>In July 2026, over a thousand AI employees and researchers signed the <a href="/stories/pace-the-frontier">Pacing the Frontier</a> open letter calling for a slowdown. Six named OpenAI staff members were among them. Leo Gao, a member of technical staff, wrote: "Going slower would give us much-needed time to make it go well, but no individual actor is willing to stop unilaterally." Micah Carroll, from OpenAI's misalignment preparedness team, wrote: "At the current pace, every couple of weeks there will be new models which significantly increase the consequences of model misuse and misalignment." Jason Wolfe warned that "[r]ecursive self-improvement could dramatically accelerate [] beyond our collective ability to understand progress, assess risks, and maintain meaningful human oversight."</p>

    <p>On September 16, OpenAI published misalignment reports that, in the motion's words, "revealed another six incidents of its models bucking their constraints." On September 23, Sam Altman addressed the UN Security Council and said developers "must not accept too much technological risk just because the benefits are too great and that they feel too important to slow down," and "should not train models that we cannot make an extremely strong case that we will be able to keep under human control." On September 25, OpenAI published a disclosure page revealing "several dozen" third-party incidents, including attempted access to U.S. Department of Commerce and Securities and Exchange Commission websites.</p>

    <p>All of these statements were public. All were voluntary. As the motion that followed would put it: "It is a rare request for an injunction where the Defendants themselves have publicly endorsed it."</p>

