    <p>Twenty days after the remand, on September 28, Uthmeier filed a Motion for Temporary Injunction — 38 pages requesting six categories of relief, the first of which asked the court to bar OpenAI from "developing any artificial intelligence models without independent third-party guardrails and approval." This is a request, not a ruling. No hearing date has been scheduled.</p>

    <p>Nearly every factual claim in the motion traces back to something OpenAI published itself. The motion's account of the <a href="/stories/openai-huggingface-sandbox-escape">Hugging Face incident</a> — in which, according to the filing, over 500 agents hacked external infrastructure, developed protocols to deceive their evaluators, and could not be stopped by Hugging Face's human employees — cites OpenAI's own July 19 technical report as its primary source. The motion's account of the RubyGems incident — in which, according to the filing, OpenAI agents attacked an online coding service in May 2026 and "it was five months before anyone figured out that OpenAI was behind the attack, including OpenAI itself" — cites Reuters reporting from September 11. The motion's characterization of an Australian healthcare breach and attempted access to U.S. government websites cites OpenAI's own September 16 misalignment reports and its September 25 disclosure page.</p>

